      "urpx:forRatePlan": {
        "@id": "gpc:eol"
      },
      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "gpc:cc-eol-2026-06-01",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "gpc:cc-eol-2026-06-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing does not carry everything its source document states. 8 parts are deferred, listed here in the same words as the filing's tariff.toml [modeling].deferrals so the two records cannot drift. (1) Fixtures counts. Each fixture charge is priced per fixture of one kind. URPX v0.5.1 names no profile property for a count of fixtures of a kind, so each fixture's MetricInput is a urpx:profileMetricInput that names no urpx:profileMetricProperty and states the kind in its name, in the pattern of Jackson EMC's Schedule S-26; a consumer supplies the counts. (2) Fuel seasons by billing month. FCR-27 prices June through September and October through May, which Schedule R's bills show is read by the billing month a bill is rendered in. The JSON-LD gates the fuel PriceDefinitions on calendar months, so usage in the weeks a billing period runs ahead of its billing month takes the other season's fuel price. (3) Fuel Cost Recovery voltage levels. FCR-27 also prices Primary Distribution and Transmission customers. Only the Secondary Distribution rates are carried, as Prices with no condition, so they apply to every customer on the schedule. (4) Municipal Franchise Fee base. MFF-11 applies to the total revenues of each bill, which include the ECCR-15 and DSM-C-15 amounts. Its multiplier Prices here are based on each fixture charge's Price and on the fuel subtotal, not on the ECCR and DSM-C multiplier Prices, so the franchise fee on those two amounts, about 0.44 percent of the base bill inside city limits, is not carried. (5) Demand Side Management schedule. The sheet applies \"the Company's effective Demand Side Management Residential or Commercial Schedule\". DSM-C-15 applies to all Commercial Customers as the Company's Rules and Regulations for Electric Service define them, and is carried here as Prices with no condition, so it applies to every customer on the schedule. A Residential Customer served on the schedule would pay DSM-R-16, 1.1969 percent, instead; the sheet names no schedule for an Industrial Customer. Neither case is carried. (6) Fixture kWh. FCR-27 is carried per kWh on a calculated MetricInput that sums each fixture's count times its Fixture Monthly kWh from Section A; its formula is text in its CalculationMethod, which URPX v0.5.1 does not evaluate. The Company may verify kWh by installing a kWh meter or a device to limit connected load, which is not carried. (7) Wattages not in the table. The Company may bill energy for Company-owned luminaires of wattages the table does not list by the sheet's formulas, Fixture Input Wattage / 1000 x 360 hours x 7.5150 cents (photo controlled) or x 720 hours x 8.6122 cents (continuous burn). Only the 46 rates the table lists are carried. (8) Construction charges and Term of Contract. Where the Company spends more than five times the estimated annual base revenue, it may collect a Contribution In Aid of Construction for the excess; a customer-owned system must provide mounting, wiring and any weatherproof enclosure for Company-supplied controls. The term is at least two years, up to five at the Company's option, with an advance payment of up to half the estimated annual revenue. None is priced per month and none is carried.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-19T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
